WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:04.716
Check distance, elevation and terrain between station and forest.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:09.421
Read metadata on missing values and observational changes.

00:00:09.882 --> 00:00:14.691
Distinguish observations, interpolated values and anomalies.

00:00:15.145 --> 00:00:19.079
The closest station is not always the most representative.

00:00:19.539 --> 00:00:23.711
A mountain forest can differ substantially from a lowland station.

00:00:24.171 --> 00:00:27.138
Missing climate values are not zeros.

00:00:27.592 --> 00:00:33.000
Record any interpolation or adjustment, together with the definition of anomalies and the

00:00:33.013 --> 00:00:36.120
reference period used to calculate them.

00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:39.424
Check dating and measurement quality.

00:00:39.886 --> 00:00:43.623
Multiple cores from one tree are not independent trees.

00:00:44.083 --> 00:00:48.077
Show annual sample depth and stability of the shared signal.

00:00:48.531 --> 00:00:53.287
A long chronology can have much weaker replication in its earliest years.

00:00:53.741 --> 00:00:59.215
A value supported by one tree is not equivalent to a value supported by many independent

00:00:59.228 --> 00:01:00.274
trees.

00:01:00.728 --> 00:01:05.530
Read sample depth alongside the climate curve and check whether the common signal remains

00:01:05.544 --> 00:01:08.860
stable as the available samples change.

00:01:09.663 --> 00:01:13.026
Choose how to handle age- and size-related trends.

00:01:13.481 --> 00:01:17.488
Flexible detrending can remove long climate variations.

00:01:17.942 --> 00:01:22.047
Test whether conclusions change under alternative processing.

00:01:22.507 --> 00:01:26.784
Detrending is not a perfect separation of climate from biology.

00:01:27.244 --> 00:01:33.093
A flexible curve can follow and remove slow climatic variation as well as age effects.

00:01:33.547 --> 00:01:38.705
Choose processing in relation to the timescales of the research question, and examine how

00:01:38.718 --> 00:01:42.344
alternative reasonable methods affect the result.

00:01:43.147 --> 00:01:47.523
A long chronology may comprise many shorter individual series.

00:01:47.978 --> 00:01:52.366
Series length and detrending constrain recoverable slow variation.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.880
This is discussed as the segment length curse.

00:01:56.333 --> 00:02:01.755
A chronology can extend for many centuries while being built from much shorter segments.

00:02:02.215 --> 00:02:07.399
Treating those segments individually can constrain the recovery of slow variation.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.366
Dating length and climatic timescale are therefore different properties.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:17.597
The relevant question is which variations survive the chosen construction and detrending

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.140
methods.

00:02:19.943 --> 00:02:22.686
Horizontal axis: ring index I.

00:02:22.811 --> 00:02:25.791
Vertical axis: seasonal mean temperature T.

00:02:25.877 --> 00:02:29.043
Estimate coefficients from paired observations.

00:02:29.498 --> 00:02:32.274
A four-year fictional teaching example.

00:02:32.735 --> 00:02:37.011
A fitted regression line does not need to pass through every observation.

00:02:37.472 --> 00:02:42.827
These four synthetic points illustrate the idea, rather than the amount of evidence required

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.123
in research.

00:02:44.577 --> 00:02:50.287
Real applications need a substantially longer overlap and checks of residuals, independence

00:02:50.393 --> 00:02:52.264
and stability.

00:02:53.067 --> 00:02:58.488
Model: estimated temperature estimated temperature equals six plus four times the ring

00:02:58.501 --> 00:02:59.601
index.

00:03:00.056 --> 00:03:01.813
I is dimensionless.

00:03:02.267 --> 00:03:07.905
The intercept is in degrees Celsius and the slope in degrees Celsius per index unit.

00:03:08.359 --> 00:03:12.267
These coefficients belong only to this synthetic example.

00:03:12.727 --> 00:03:17.431
An index of one gives an estimated temperature of ten degrees Celsius.

00:03:17.885 --> 00:03:23.214
An increase of one tenth in the index changes the estimate by four tenths of a degree.

00:03:23.675 --> 00:03:27.280
This is not an equation for raw widths in millimetres.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:32.267
Changing the predictor definition or standardization changes the model to which the

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.532
coefficients belong.

00:03:35.335 --> 00:03:40.928
Calibration: 2001–2004, used to estimate coefficients.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:47.699
Verification: 2005–2007, evaluated with the equation fixed.

00:03:48.153 --> 00:03:51.127
Both are short fictional periods for teaching.

00:03:51.587 --> 00:03:56.725
The first four years are used for calibration and the next three for verification.

00:03:57.179 --> 00:04:02.778
Those short periods are chosen for arithmetic, not as a recommended research design.

00:04:03.232 --> 00:04:08.916
Adjacent years may be related, so randomly splitting individual rows does not necessarily

00:04:08.929 --> 00:04:12.360
provide an independent time-series test.

00:04:13.163 --> 00:04:17.842
Season, variable and processing choices are part of model building.

00:04:18.297 --> 00:04:23.415
Adjusting after viewing test results calls for a new independent evaluation.

00:04:23.876 --> 00:04:26.843
Record the time split and search procedure.

00:04:27.297 --> 00:04:32.231
Fixing the regression coefficients is not enough if the season was already chosen using the

00:04:32.244 --> 00:04:33.771
entire record.

00:04:34.231 --> 00:04:39.080
Evaluate the procedure that selected the model as well as the final equation.

00:04:39.534 --> 00:04:44.468
An unsuccessful test is useful evidence about the limits of the model, not a result to

00:04:44.481 --> 00:04:46.036
conceal.
